    "q-161186": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161186",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
