    "q-161201": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161201",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,xz,\\,z+{y}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?",
      "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,xz,\\,z+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,xz,\\,z+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+0-(z+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-z}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{{x}^{2}-z}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-z\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
